    "lawCode": "Add §485-v, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4855\n\nSPONSOR: REICHLIN-MELNICK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property tax law, in relation to a residential\nrevaluation exemption\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to help mitigate the effects of a\ntown-wide revaluation by giving a town the ability to adopt a two-year\nphase-in option for assessment increases on certain residential proper-\nties.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the Real Property Tax law by adding § 485-v\nproviding for a Residential Revaluation exemption. After a public hear-\ning, the governing body of an assessing unit may provide a residential\nrevaluation exemption in the first full year if a full value revalu-\nation. This section further provides that a property is eligible for the\nexemption if:\n   1. the property is a residential property\n   2. the property is eligible to receive the STAR exemption or the\npersonal income tax STAR credit\n   3. in any given year, the owner(s) receiving the exemption match the\nowner(s) on the assessment roll, and\n   4. the property does not have any delinquent taxes as of the\n   taxable status date. (b)\n\nUnder this section, an assessing unit may further limit the eligibility\nto eligible residential properties whose full value increase exceeds a\nset percentage threshold as specified in their local law.\n\nThe Residential Revaluation Exemption shall be computed with respect to\na percentage of the \"exemption base\", which is defined in the bill. The\nexemption can be up to 66 per centum of the exemption base in year one\nand 33 per centum in year two.\n\nAny increase in the assessment due to physical changes in the year\nfollowing the implementation roll shall not be eligible for exemption.\nAdditionally, if the property granted an exemption transfers ownership\nor ceases to meet eligibility, the exemption shall be discontinued, at\nwhich point the assessor shall mail a notice to the owner\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York State does not require assessing units to conduct regular reas-\nsessments. As a result, reassessment activity is inconsistent across the\nState. In many instances, assessments are out of date and do not accu-\nrately reflect the actual value of properties, causing some taxpayers to\npay too much in taxes and others to pay too little.  In an effort to\nmake assessments better reflect current property values, many towns are\ndeciding to complete town-wide property reassessments. Some of these\ntowns had not been reassessed for many decades, and therefore updating\nthe assessment rolls can often cause some owners to experience severe\nincreases in their property taxes.  Although these increases may be in\nthe interest of fairness, they can be jarring and sudden. This legis-\nlation will help mitigate the impacts of these increases by allowing a\ntown to adopt a local law, after a public hearing, to phase-in the\nincreased assessments that some residents will experience. It will also\nallow homeowners to prepare financially for their new, increased tax\nliability.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: S.1551-A - amended and recommitted to local government\n2017-18: S.5547-A - amended and recommitted to local government\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere are no fiscal implications to the State in association with the\npassage of this legislation.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
